From f11d8d132b82a92fd089c74ceef396723f82fdfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuubOS <nuubos@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 21:05:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] AICA thread: fair lock, up to 1.5 frames behind
 (nuubOS)

aica_lock (SH4 register accesses against the AICA thread's samples) was
a std::mutex: the SH4 thread slept in the kernel ~18000 times a second in
Shenmue II, ~6 ms of every frame that renders, because the AICA thread
took it back for the next sample before the sleeper woke. It is now a
ticket spinlock (fair, yields only if the holder is not running).

At vblank the emulation thread waited for every AICA tick asked for; in
frames where the game only waits, the SH4 got there early and idled
~5 ms for the AICA, which had time to spare in the next frame. It now
waits only while the AICA is more than 32 ticks (~23 ms) behind
(libAICA_SyncLag); save states, reset and unload still wait for all
(emu_hold_synced, reset_requested). The SH4 sees the AICA up to that
late. Samples go through a single-writer single-reader ring and each
retro_run sends what has been made (about, not exactly, a frame's 735);
RetroArch's rate control evens it out.

Upstream: not submitted (nuubOS H700 tuning).
